            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Hindi for what's going on, then the Hindi for have you eaten."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: भूखा",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "भूखा (bhūkhā) — \"hungry\". A woman says भूखी."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is भूख, \"hunger\", made into a description. मैं भूखा (bhūkhā) हूँ — \"I am hungry\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "भूखा (bhūkhā) is a tadbhava, a word inherited and reshaped by sound change. It comes from bubhukṣaka, \"hungry\", a form CDIAL reconstructs from its descendants (CDIAL 9284). It is built on the Sanskrit \"wish to eat\", bubhukṣā."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hunger, made into how you are."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
